  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nGuidance is a bit mixed on fog coverage through early this morning, \nbut given the high dewpoints and light winds, it still remains a \npossibility for much of the area. River valleys are the most \nlikely and also along the coast...but it's also possible the \nmarine fog/stratus seen on satellite could largely remain \noffshore as the low-level wind direction is not very favorable \nfor bringing it ashore, except maybe the Midcoast and around \nPenobscot Bay.\n\nFog should quickly clear up by mid-morning with another hot and \nhumid day ahead. 850mb temperatures of +17C to +19C support highs \nreaching the upper 80s to lower 90s south of the mountains, and \ncombining these with dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s will push \nheat indices into the 90s for much of northern New England. The peak \nof the heat is expected to be across southeastern NH extending into \ninterior western Maine and the western ME coast, where a Heat \nAdvisory remains in effect for heat indices expected to be in \nthe 95F-100F range. We should see some increase in diurnal cu in\nthe afternoon, but the 500mb pattern indicates weak shortwave \nridging building in, which should add some subsidence to keep \nany convection very isolated much of the day. This weak ridge \ndoes begin to shift east toward this evening, so we'll see if \nany convection develops in NH or moves in from the west, similar\nto last evening.\n\nFor tonight, temperatures remain mild ranging from the mid 60s \nto around 70. Latest guidance is in reasonably good agreement in\nshortwave energy approaching from the west overnight, which \nwill result in a chance of showers along with an increase in \nclouds. Valley fog is again possible, but the clouds may end up \nhindering this potential. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm and humid airmass remains in place for Saturday with\nhigher chances for showers and storms with better defined \nshortwaves crossing northern New England. 00Z guidance is \ncoming into better agreement with scattered showers possible in \nthe morning with the same wave that approaches the region Friday\nnight. A quicker exit to the east may bring a break and a \nbetter chance for some degrees of clearing and instability to \nbuild going into the afternoon hours for showers and \nthunderstorms to develop with the arrival of the next shortwave.\nThe latest NAMnest, RAP, and HRRR are generally supportive of \nthis thinking, although the amount of instability/clear is still\na bit uncertain (and to that extent high temperatures). If \nenough instability is gained, a few strong to severe storms will\nbe possible given 30-35 kt of deep layer shear. PWATs are still\nexpected to be around 1.75\" so rain rates could again be quite \nefficient, and localized flooding is possible in areas that \nreceive persistent or multiple rounds of convection. Back to \nhigh temperatures...model consensus is mostly in the 80s for \nmost areas, although parts of NH could still approach 90. Once \nshowers and storms some to an end Saturday evening, fog may \ndevelop Saturday night with lows mainly in the 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter mainly dry days on Sunday and Monday, shower and \nthunderstorm chances will increase Monday night into Tuesday as \na wave of low pressure and associated frontal boundary crosses \nfrom the north. The timing of this disturbance as well as the \none preceding it will play a large role in determining if there\nis any potential for severe storms/flash flooding but as of \nnow, atmospheric ingredients are not appearing to be overly \nsupportive. High temperatures on Monday and Tuesday will \ngenerally be into the 70s across the north with 80s south.\n\nTemperatures will trend a little cooler through the remainder of the \nweek, although not far from seasonable averages. A few additional \nweak disturbances will bring the potential for scattered showers at \ntimes, although no day currently looks to be a washout. Given the \nongoing dry conditions in much of the region, rainfall would be \nlargely beneficial.",
